{
  "dom_id": "step-e22722",
  "name": "Example: Interpreting Addition Using Absolute Value",
  "problem": "Let $p=-3$ and $q=8.$ What point on the number line corresponds to $p+q?$",
  "worked_solution": "The value of $p+q$ is the number located a distance $|q|$ from $p$ on the number line, \n\n- moving to the *right* if $q$ is *positive*, and\n\n- moving to the *left* if $q$ is *negative*.\n\nIn our situation:\n\n- We start at $p = -3$ on the number line.\n\n- Here, $q = 8$, so the distance we move is $|q| = 8.$\n\n- Because $q$ is *positive*, we move $8$ units to the *right* from $-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14152428/assets/b44a40ef3278f0b31899b7a98695a92e332859b5d0f7badb82a241b1bcbef022.png)\n\nThis point corresponds to the number\n\n$$\n-3 + 8 = 5.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
